From e3995c02465f51308d6e6071a38e6a0496665287 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jolyon Brown <jolyon@limilo.com>
Date: Thu, 12 Nov 2026 19:46:12 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 14/28] Fix exponential walks in two native code block checks

Two checks in the native code generator walk the flow graph recursively
and visited a block once per path instead of once. A run of N
independent conditionals has 2^N paths through it, so the compile time
doubled or tripled with every conditional added (IsSameRegisterSource
at every native optimisation level, CanJoinEntryLoadStoreZP above -O0).

IsSameRegisterSource (does the A, X or Y register already hold the
value this load gives, on every path into the block; for
PartialBackwardValuePropagation and BackwardValuePropagation) walks all
predecessors and stops at the first failure. It now keeps the (block,
loop count) pairs it has verified. A pair seen again returns true, as
it did the first time, because a failure would have ended the walk; the
loop count is part of the key because it changes the answer at a loop
head.

CanJoinEntryLoadStoreZP (can a zero page copy move up into the blocks
in front of a join) has the same shape and gets a visited set per
check. Its result depends only on the block, and it still sets mChecked
on every block it reaches, so DoJoinEntryLoadStoreZP changes the same
blocks as before.

The test has one function per walk, with 16 and 24 conditionals, called
with more than one argument value so that the arguments are not folded
into them. Without the fix each function takes a second or more to
compile in the native configurations; with it the file compiles in
well under a second in all ten configurations.

This is the part of the change proposed for upstream main (Fix
exponential walks and a stack overflow in native code block checks)
that applies to v1.32.273; the third walk of that change does not exist
here.

Co-Authored-By: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
